2. Java and ASR exposure for CAB
Removing Java where it is never used cuts 78% of the Java attack surface with no business impact. I confirmed the numbers with two independent queries.
• Java: installed on 23 devices, used on 5. The 18 unused installs are 4–8 years out of date (Java 8 from 2019–2021, end-of-life Java 14). Defender flags 287 vulnerabilities (1 Critical, 52 High) on 5 of them. The one vulnerable device that uses Java carries 334 and should be patched first.
• ASR already blocks the script attack path: JavaScript/VBScript downloads, obfuscated scripts, Office child processes, LSASS credential theft (98,000 blocks in 30 days, consistent with normal work-week activity).
• Gaps: 3 ASR rules still in Audit (prevalence/age, Office macro Win32 calls, Adobe Reader). A new pool of virtual desktop devices, reporting since Nov 12, audits LSASS instead of blocking and may be outside the standard policy; it may also be part of the Secure Score drop.
Proposal (CAB doc delivered): uninstall Java from the 18 unused devices; patch the 5 that need it; pilot the 3 Audit rules in Block on IT; add a Sentinel detection for Java or scripts launching remote tools; review Teams external chat separately.
3. Detection and monitoring
• External risk feed in Sentinel: I reviewed the analytics rule. It is well built (one incident per alert, two-way status sync through three playbooks, no duplicates on updates). Two gaps: no entity mapping, so incidents can't correlate with Defender, and static MITRE tags (Phishing/Masquerading) on every alert type, so vulnerability alerts score as low priority. The feed doesn't actively scan, so each "vulnerable service" finding needs validation in Defender. I proposed tactics per alert type (vulnerabilities to T1190) and entity mapping, tested as a disabled copy through CAB.
• Workspace Usage Report workbook: I configured and enabled it for SOC response times against SLA (High 1h / Medium 8h / Low 24h, plus median and P90), table ingestion and cost, latency and health checks.
• Content Hub: I identified 16 Sentinel solutions with updates (now 15) and prepared a batched update plan with a rule baseline first.
• Leaked-credential follow-up: I designed a watchlist and detection rule so a leaked address alerts if it is ever created or used, pending approval.
• Anti-ransomware: I drafted the vendor root-cause case for the Nov 12 incident, explained an agent stop event during an incident window (normal shutdown pattern), and triaged a file-tampering event to a signed vendor support tool (likely false positive, exclusion candidate for team review).
4. Phishing simulation and planning
Phishing simulation pilot (IT): 99 simulations, 9 failures (9.1%), 17 reports (17.2%), risk score 77/100. AI-powered scenarios have the highest failure rate (19%), which supports the deepfake and voice phishing plan.
• Gap found: 0 of the 9 failed users have completed remediation training. Likely cause: remediation points to the platform's own training app, which isn't enabled for employees because training runs through the HR learning system. Fix options: enable employee sign-in for remediation, or push remediation modules into the learning system.
• Gap found: repeat failures aren't yet escalated to managers, which leadership asked for.
• I mapped the full rollout as a flowchart and drafted scenarios based on our real attacks (fake help desk in Teams, fake legal notice).
Planning and governance
• Security Program Implementation Plan: owners, RACI, change control and a timeline for Purview, ISO and the phishing program.
• Agent 365 and Perception (requested by Ken): fit assessment in progress. Perception's triage agent appears to address our 24/7 triage need with agentic automation, faster response and humans approving critical actions, which is the scale leadership is investing in.
